<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Plotinus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 204–270</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Plotinus was a Greek-speaking philosopher, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, who studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammonius-saccas">Ammonius Saccas</a> and from 244 taught in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. His treatises, edited by his pupil Porphyry as the Enneads, describe all reality as flowing from a single source, the One. He is regarded as the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonism</a>, which shaped later pagan, Christian and Islamic thought.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plotinus.jpg" alt="Plotinus" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plotinos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
